/**

@page page_exahype2_solver_manipulate_solver_steps Manipulate an existing solver's steps (e.g. inserting additional sweeps)

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE 2's numerical schemes do not hard-code a sequence of grid traversals.
Each scheme describes one time step as a task graph, and the task graph
compiler maps this graph onto grid sweeps later on. You can therefore alter
what a solver does per time step, e.g. postprocess its solution after each
update or add a step of your own, without touching the numerical scheme. You
modify the task graph before it is compiled.

This page describes the mechanism. The tutorial
@ref tutorials_exahype2_20_additional-solver-steps walks through a hands-on
example, a density floor for the Euler equations, for both Finite Volumes and
Runge-Kutta Finite Differences. The enforcement of the CCZ4 constraints, which
the numerical schemes offer through the flag enforce_ccz4_constraints, uses
the same mechanism.


# From a solver to task graphs

Every exahype2.api.Solver contributes to three task graphs
(peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph), one per algorithmic phase: the setup of the
simulation, the time stepping, and the plotting. When the project generates
the code, it calls each solver's generate_solver_realisation(), which
proceeds in four steps:

1. The grid construction adds its tasks to the setup graph.
2. The numerical scheme adds its tasks to the time stepping graph.
3. The plotter, if any, adds its tasks to the plot graph.
4. The solver's postprocess_task_graphs() receives all three graphs.

Only afterwards, the project hands the graphs over to the task graph
compiler. postprocess_task_graphs() hence is the place where you can
manipulate a solver's steps: All tasks are known, but none of them has been
translated into a grid traversal yet.

The compiler decides how many grid traversals a graph needs. A task which
you add might be fused into an existing traversal, or it might require a
traversal of its own, i.e. an additional sweep. This depends on the data
that the task reads and writes, its dependencies and the compiler chosen. You
do not have to decide this yourself. However, you have to declare correctly
which data a task uses, as the compiler relies on these declarations.


# Tags

To insert a step after a solution update, you have to find the tasks which
update the solution. You do not want to know how a particular numerical
scheme builds its graph, though. The schemes therefore label the relevant
tasks.

## Tags in general

Every peano4.api.taskgraph.Task carries a set tags of plain strings. A task can
carry arbitrarily many tags at the same time. Tags are global labels: Nobody
constructs a tag object, you add a string to the set:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
task = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name="My task", entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL, tags={"mylibrary.MyLabel"})
task.tags.add(exahype2.api.Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The task graph compilers ignore tags. They exist only so that the code which
builds a graph can tell other code what a task does.
peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.find_tasks(tag) returns all tasks of a graph which
carry a certain tag, in the order in which they have been added to the graph.
If you pass several tags, i.e. find_tasks(tag_a, tag_b), it returns all tasks
which carry at least one of them, each task once. Prefix your own tags, e.g.
with the name of your library, so they cannot clash with the tags of others.

## The solution update tags

exahype2.api.Solver defines two tags, i.e. two string constants:

- exahype2.api.Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag: Each numerical scheme of ExaHyPE
  adds this tag to each task of its time stepping graph which writes a new
  solution.
- exahype2.api.Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTag: Runge-Kutta schemes
  compute intermediate solutions, i.e. the inputs of further stages, before
  they compute the new solution. They add this tag to each task which writes
  such an intermediate solution.

The distinction allows you to pick: A step which should act on the solution
only, e.g. some statistics, searches for SolutionUpdateTaskTag. A step which
should act on every intermediate solution, too, e.g. the enforcement of
constraints or a density floor, searches for both tags.

A tag only says what a task does. It does not say which data the task
writes or which cells it updates. If you insert a step, you have to know the
numerical scheme you work with: The table below lists, per scheme, the tagged
tasks, the patch they write, and the guard, i.e. the C++ boolean expression
which selects the cells that a task updates.

## Which tasks are tagged

| Numerical scheme | Tag | Tagged tasks | Patch | Guard |
|------------------|-----|--------------|-------|-------|
| exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler | SolutionUpdateTaskTag | "Update cell" | solver._patch | solver.grid_selector.createCellGuard() |
| exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithOptimisticExplicitEuler | SolutionUpdateTaskTag | "Update cell" (traditional branch) and "Insert patch interior" (optimistic branch) | solver._patch | the cell guard combined with the condition that picks the branch, see below |
| exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta | IntermediateSolutionUpdateTag | "Prepare stage k+1" for every stage but the last one | solver._stage_input | solver.grid_selector.createCellGuard() |
| exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta | SolutionUpdateTaskTag | "Complete time step" after the last stage | solver._patch | solver.grid_selector.createCellGuard() |

All task names carry the solver's name as prefix, i.e. read
"{solver}_Update cell". A project with several solvers has several tagged
tasks per graph, so filter for the tasks of your solver.

The optimistic Finite Volumes compute the new solution in one of two tasks,
depending on the cell. The scheme picks the branch per cell through a guard
which reads the cell's eigenvalue and time step sizes. A step that you insert
after both tasks with the plain cell guard runs twice for the cells of the
optimistic branch, and its first run sees a half-updated patch. This is fine
for steps which you can apply twice, such as a density floor, but it is
wasteful. The scheme's own constraint enforcement (enforce_ccz4_constraints)
uses the branch guards instead.

The Runge-Kutta scheme tags the writer of each stage. A step that you insert
after each task with either tag runs after each stage. Consequently, the
right-hand side of the next stage is evaluated on postprocessed data only.
This is what you need, e.g., to enforce constraints or a density floor, as an
unphysical stage could crash the flux evaluation. A step that you insert
after the tasks with SolutionUpdateTaskTag only runs once per time step.


# The contract

The tags are a contract between the numerical schemes and any code which
manipulates their graphs.

The numerical schemes promise to

- tag all tasks which write a new solution with SolutionUpdateTaskTag, and
  all tasks which write a new stage input with
  IntermediateSolutionUpdateTag; and
- not read the outcome of a writer within the writer's own task. A scheme
  must compute eigenvalues or the projection onto the faces in a subsequent
  task instead. This is why the Runge-Kutta scheme splits each stage into
  "Prepare stage k" and "Project stage k onto faces": Postprocessing has to
  fit in between, so that the neighbours see postprocessed data only.

Code which inserts steps has to

- declare all data that a new task reads or writes via use_cell(),
  use_face() or use_vertex(), including the patch and all data which the
  guard reads;
- use the guard of the tagged task;
- insert the task via peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.insert_after() instead of
  adding a plain dependency.

insert_after(anchor, task) adds task to the graph and makes it depend on
anchor. In addition, it redirects all tasks which have depended on anchor so
far to the new task. Your task hence runs after the update but before
anything which used to follow the update, such as the projection onto the
faces. A plain task.add_dependency(anchor) would allow these successors to
run before your task, i.e. on data which has not been postprocessed yet.
As insert_after() redirects the successors which exist when you call it,
insert your task once the graph is complete.

If you insert several steps after the same anchor, chain them: use the task
which you have just inserted as the anchor of the next one.


# Inserting steps

## Inserting a step yourself

Inserting a step always follows the same pattern: Call find_tasks(), and,
for each task returned, create a new task and add it to the graph via
insert_after(). The solver itself does not know which patch a tagged task
writes or which guard it uses, so the insertion is up to the code which knows
the numerical scheme. The natural place is postprocess_task_graphs() in a
subclass of exahype2.api.Solver, as all tasks are known there. The sketch
below adds a density floor after each update of a Runge-Kutta scheme:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
class MySolver(exahype2.api.Solver):
  def postprocess_task_graphs(self, exahype2_project, peano_project, setup_simulation, time_step, plot):
    super().postprocess_task_graphs(exahype2_project, peano_project, setup_simulation, time_step, plot)
    for anchor in time_step.find_tasks(
      exahype2.api.Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag,
      exahype2.api.Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTag,
    ):
      if not anchor.name.startswith(self.name + "_"):
        continue
      patch = self._patch if exahype2.api.Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag in anchor.tags else self._stage_input
      task = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(
        name   = f"{self.name}_Density floor after {anchor.name}",
        entity = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL,
      )
      task.use_cell(patch, peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.DataAccess.READ_AND_WRITE)
      task.add_action_set(
        toolbox.blockstructured.api.ApplyPointWiselyToPatch(
          patch               = patch,
          implementation      = "Q[0] = std::max(Q[0], 0.9);",
          guard               = self.grid_selector.createCellGuard(),
          additional_includes = self._get_default_includes() + self.user_solver_includes,
        )
      )
      time_step.insert_after(anchor, task)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

toolbox.blockstructured.api.ApplyPointWiselyToPatch runs a C++ snippet for each
volume, or grid point, of a patch. The snippet may read and write Q, i.e. all
unknowns and auxiliary variables of one volume, and read the volume's
position x. Your step can use any other action set as well, e.g. one which
needs a halo or works on faces or vertices.

Declare the patch with READ_AND_WRITE even if your step only reads it:
the access mode keeps the data alive for the tasks which follow. Drop
IntermediateSolutionUpdateTag from the find_tasks() call if your step should
run once per time step only, i.e. not after each Runge-Kutta stage.

The routine receives the setup and the plot graph, too. The tags are only
used within the time stepping graph, though. If you want to alter the other
graphs, you have to identify the tasks of interest yourself, e.g. via their
names, or add tasks which do not depend on any existing task.

## Enforcing the CCZ4 constraints

The numerical schemes know their patches and guards. They hence can insert
steps themselves. All schemes listed above accept a flag
enforce_ccz4_constraints, which is False by default. If you set it, the
scheme inserts a task which applies exagrype::ccz4::enforceCCZ4constraints()
to each volume after each tagged task, using the patch and the guard of this
task (exahype2.api.Solver.Solver.NumericalScheme._insert_ccz4_constraint_enforcement()):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver.numerical_scheme = exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithOptimisticExplicitEuler(
  solver,
  patch_size,
  enforce_ccz4_constraints = True,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The task names read "{solver}_Enforce CCZ4 constraints after {task}". Use the
flag with exagrype.api.CCZ4Solver only, as the routine requires the CCZ4
unknowns. If you run the generator with TEXT or VISUALISATION logging, it
prints or plots the time stepping graph including these tasks.


# Writing a new numerical scheme

If you write a numerical scheme of your own, i.e. a subclass of
exahype2.api.Solver.NumericalScheme, you have to keep the scheme's side of the
contract. Otherwise, every step which relies on the tags will silently miss
your updates.

- Tag each task which writes a new solution with
  task.tags.add(Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag).
- Tag each task which writes the input of a further stage, i.e. an
  intermediate solution, with
  task.tags.add(Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTag).
- Do not read the written patch in the same task. Move eigenvalue
  computations, the projection onto the faces and similar operations into
  a subsequent task.
- Document which patch each tagged task writes and which guard it uses.
- If you want to support enforce_ccz4_constraints, pass the flag to
  NumericalScheme.__init__() and call _insert_ccz4_constraint_enforcement()
  for each tagged task once your graph is complete.

*/
